      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The role of laparoscopic repair for femoral hernia has not been clearly defined, although the advantages of this technique for repair of inguinal hernia are well recognized."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to assess the outcome of laparoscopic total extraperitoneal (TEP) repair of femoral hernia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Case records of patients who had laparoscopic TEP repair of femoral hernia between 1994 and 2002 were reviewed retrospectively. Patients' demographic details, presentation, operative details, and follow-up information were gathered from the clinical records. Postoperative complications, chronic pain, and recurrence of the hernia were assessed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Fifteen patients, 10 males and 5 females, with a mean age of 55 years (range, 33-84 years) underwent laparoscopic TEP femoral hernia repair. Fourteen patients (93.3%) had a primary femoral hernia, and one had a recurrent femoral hernia. In 9 (60%) patients the hernia was irreducible but not obstructed. There were no postoperative complications or chronic pain. One patient (7%) with a small (11 x 6 cm) mesh developed an inguinal recurrence."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Laparoscopic TEP repair is a suitable technique for repair of femoral hernia, including irreducible but not obstructed femoral hernias."
        }
      ]},
